    "narrative": "The path to a productive Tuesday for Ghost Ride the WHIP relies on capitalizing on clear platoon advantages and favorable park factors. **Tyler Soderstrom** leads the charge with a 72.72 efficiency score, bolstered by a juicy matchup against Me. Kelly and a significant 19% boost derived from the opposing starter's profile. He is joined in the top tier by **Mickey Moniak** (69.85) and **James Wood** (69.20), both of whom benefit from exceptional park environments and prime batting order positioning. Specifically, **James Wood**\u2019s elite 0.425 xwOBA at the top of the lineup makes him a cornerstone play, while **Mickey Moniak** leverages a 14% boost from a favorable platoon split to maximize his ceiling in a high-run-environment stadium.\n\nStrategic depth remains critical, as evidenced by our decision to keep **Josh Naylor** on the bench despite his solid 59.33 projection. While **Josh Naylor** provided a respectable floor, he ultimately fell short due to a poor BvP track record\u2014a 7% penalty over 28 plate appearances\u2014and a negative park factor adjustment that dropped him just below the threshold of our starters. We are prioritizing the higher efficiency ratings of **Austin Riley** (63.64) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (64.25), both of whom are positioned to exploit soft pitching matchups. Meanwhile, caution is advised regarding **Seiya Suzuki** (64.43); while his underlying metrics are strong, the \"Wind In\" warning creates a non-trivial risk of flyball suppression that tempers our expectations. Similarly, we are monitoring **Corbin Carroll** (58.95) closely; he remains a start based on his sprint speed necessity, but he faces a tighter window against Jump (L) where his platoon disadvantage is compounded by the opponent\u2019s ability to limit stolen base opportunities.",
